  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14099242/assets/bedd89f5d2804ee3880e6b07483043b61681e73b5d2b04a8d1487c8fd8b225ca.png)\n\nThe shaded regions above represent the truth sets of the predicates $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y),$ defined over the universal set ${R}^{2}.$ Which of the following is the truth set of $P\u2228Q?$",
  "worked_solution": "The truth set of $P\u2228Q$ represents the *union* of the truth sets of $P(x,y)$ and $Q(x,y):$\n\n$$\n{T}_{P\u2228Q}={T}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}\n$$\n\nTherefore, to get the truth set of $P(x,y)\u2228Q(x,y),$ we take all the points that belong to ${T}_{P}$ or ${T}_{Q},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14099242/assets/319ee2064f6f31da6d7851a4446aff1295dd2a8360cff2318c43eac7b279291a.png)\n\n**Note:** The points where the *dashed* line meets the *solid* curve belong to ${T}_{P}\\cup {T}_{Q}$ since these points lie in ${T}_{Q}.$",
